# api-design (Cursor)

Reference conventions for consistent, developer-friendly REST APIs.

## Resource design

URLs are nouns, plural, kebab-case, no verbs: `GET /api/v1/users`,
`GET /api/v1/users/:id/orders`. Actions that don't map to CRUD use a verb
sparingly as a sub-resource: `POST /api/v1/orders/:id/cancel`. Avoid
`/getUsers`, singular resources, snake_case, verbs in nested paths.

## HTTP methods and status codes

GET/PUT/DELETE are idempotent, POST/PATCH generally aren't. Use status
codes semantically: 200 (read), 201 (created, with `Location` header), 204
(no body), 400/422 (validation), 401/403 (auth), 404, 409 (conflict), 429
(rate limit), 500/502/503 for server-side. Never return 200 for everything
with a `success: false` body — use the real status code.

## Response format

Success: `{ "data": {...} }`. Collections add `meta` (pagination) and
`links`. Errors: `{ "error": { "code", "message", "details": [...] } }`
with field-level detail for validation failures.

## Pagination

Offset (`?page=2&per_page=20`) for admin dashboards/small datasets/search
results where users expect page numbers. Cursor-based (`?cursor=...`) for
infinite scroll, feeds, and public APIs — consistent performance and stable
under concurrent inserts, but can't jump to an arbitrary page.

## Filtering, sorting, search

Equality (`?status=active`), comparison via bracket notation
(`?price[gte]=10`), comma-separated multi-value, dot notation for nested
fields, `-` prefix on `sort` for descending, `q=` for full-text, `fields=`
for sparse fieldsets.

## Auth

Bearer token or API key in headers, never in the URL. Check both
authentication (who) and authorization (can this user touch this
resource) — resource-level ownership checks and role-based checks are
different concerns, do both where relevant.

## Rate limiting

Return `X-RateLimit-*` headers and `429` + `Retry-After` when exceeded.
Tier limits by trust level (anonymous < authenticated < premium <
internal).

## Versioning

Prefer URL path versioning (`/api/v1/`) over header versioning — explicit,
cacheable, easy to route. Don't version until you need to; maintain at most
2 active versions; announce deprecation with a `Sunset` header before
returning `410 Gone`. Additive changes (new fields/params/endpoints) don't
need a new version; removing/renaming fields, changing types, URL
structure, or auth method does.

## Before shipping an endpoint, check

Naming conventions, correct HTTP method, real status codes, schema
validation on input, standard error format, pagination on list endpoints,
auth + authorization, rate limiting, no leaked internals (stack
traces/SQL errors), consistent naming with existing endpoints, docs
updated (OpenAPI/Swagger).
